<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pedubast I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">9th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century-bce">9th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pedubast I was a king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in the late 9th century BCE, usually counted as the founder of the Twenty-third Dynasty. Around 837 BCE, amid conflict within the Twenty-second Dynasty, he proclaimed himself king and was recognised at Thebes, rivalling <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/takelot-ii">Takelot II</a> and his son, the High Priest Osorkon. His rise marked the splitting of Egypt among several Libyan royal lines.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pedubast-i.jpg" alt="Pedubast I" width="320" height="479" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Moisés Robledo · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Usermaatra-Padibastet_Sculpture.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
